The Blessed One is like a blooming lotus (paduma); the Dhamma is like the honey produced in that lotus; the Saṅgha is like the swarm of bees that enjoy that honey. In this way, this Triple Refuge should be illustrated with metaphors.

By this much, the matrix (mātikā) for the explanation of the meaning, which was set forth previously in the four verses beginning with "By whom, where, when, and why was the Triple Refuge spoken?", has been clearly explained in terms of its meaning.

## Within the Commentary on the Khuddakapāṭha named Paramatthajotikā,
## the Explanation of the Triple Refuge is finished.
## 2. Sikkhāpada-vaṇṇanā
## The Matrix of the Text of the Training Rules

Having thus shown the entrance into the Dispensation (sāsana) through the taking of the refuges, the following matrix is now stated to explain the meaning of the text of the training rules (sikkhāpada), which are set forth to show those training rules in which a lay follower (upāsaka) or a monk who has entered the Dispensation should first train:

"By whom, where, when, and why these training rules were spoken—having stated that method, and having made a definition by way of common and specific characteristics;

Defining what is a fault by nature (lokavajja) and what is a fault by prescription (paṇṇattivajja), and having made that definition, [the explanation proceeds] by way of the meaning of the words and syllables;

By way of clarifying the commonality of all those that are common, and then by way of illustrating the specific meaning of the first five [precepts];

By way of being single or multiple starting with the taking of life (pāṇātipāta), by way of the different ways of taking an object, and likewise by way of being of great blame;

By way of effort (payoga), factors (aṅga), and origination (samuṭṭhāna), by way of feeling (vedanā), root (mūla), and course of action (kamma), by way of abstention (virati), and by way of fruit—thus should the decision be understood."

91:
In this Maṅgala Sutta and other Sutta texts of a similar nature, the sense of continuous duration (accantasaṃyoga) is appropriate. This is because when the Blessed One taught this Sutta or any other Sutta, He dwelt entirely in the dwelling of compassion (karuṇāvihāra) throughout that duration. Therefore, it should be understood that the accusative case (upayoga-vacana) is used here to clarify that meaning. Thus it is said:

"Depending on various meanings, in teachings other than the Suttas,
the word 'samaya' is spoken in the locative and instrumental cases;
but here, that word 'samaya' is spoken in the accusative case."

The name "Bhagavā" (The Blessed One) is a term of great respect and reverence for the Buddha, who is the most excellent of beings due to His distinguished qualities. As it is said:

"The word 'Bhagavā' is the best word; the word 'Bhagavā' is the supreme word.
He is endowed with qualities worthy of respect and reverence;
therefore, He is called 'Bhagavā'."

Names are of four kinds: situational (āvatthika), characteristic-based (liṅgika), qualities-based (nemittaka), and arbitrary (adhiccasamuppanna). 'Adhiccasamuppanna' means naming according to one's wish. Among these four, names such as 'calf', 'steer', or 'bull' are called situational names. Names such as 'one with a staff', 'one with an umbrella', 'one with a crest', or 'one with a trunk (elephant)' are called characteristic-based names. Names such as 'one with the three knowledges' or 'one with the six direct knowledges' are called qualities-based names. Names like 'Sirivaḍḍhaka' or 'Dhanavaḍḍhaka', which occur without regard for the literal meaning of the words, are called arbitrary names. This name "Bhagavā," however, is a qualities-based (nemittaka) name. It was not given by His mother Mahāmāyā, nor by King Suddhodana, nor by His eighty thousand relatives; nor was it given by special deities such as Sakka or Santusita. As the Venerable Sāriputta Thera taught: "The name 'Bhagavā' was not given by His mother... that name 'Bhagavā' is a designation realized at the end of the attainment of the fruit of Arahatship."

To clarify the qualities of that name which is based on attributes, this verse is recited:

"Because He possesses the six glories (bhagī), frequents the Dhamma (bhajī), possesses shares of merits (bhāgī), and is a divider of the Dhamma (vibhattavā);
because He has destroyed defilements (bhagga), is a teacher (garu), and has reached the end of perfections (bhāgyavā);
because His mind is well-developed through many methods,
and because He has gone to the end of existence, He is called 'Bhagavā'."

The meaning of this term should be understood in the same manner as stated in the Niddesa and other texts.

This is another method of explanation:

"He has reached the end of perfections (bhāgyavā), has broken the defilements (bhaggavā),
is endowed with the six glories (yutto bhagehi), and is a divider of the Dhamma (vibhattavā);
He frequents the supreme states (bhattavā), and has vomited out the three realms of existence (vantagamano);
for these reasons, He is called 'Bhagavā'."
